Fix constants $C_0\ge1$, $c_0>0$, and $L\ge1$. There are constants $B_0,K,C\ge1$ and $n_1$, depending only on these three constants, with the following property. Let $n\ge n_1$, let $(X,\mu)$ be an atomless finite measure space, and let $(C_i)_{i\in I}$ be an indexed list of measurable sets with $0<c_i:=\mu(C_i)<\infty$ and $1\le m:=|I|\le n^2$. Set $$\bar c_i=\min(c_i,1/2),\qquad s_i=-\log\bar c_i,$$ and let $d$ be a pseudometric on $I$. For nonempty $S\subseteq I$, write $$r(S)=\min_{j\in S}\max_{i\in S}d(i,j)^2,$$ and choose an index $i(S)\in S$ minimizing the uncapped measure $c_i$. Assume that $$\label{eq:block-affinity-input}
 \frac{\mu(C_i\cap C_j)}{\sqrt{\bar c_i\bar c_j}}
 \le C_0e^{-c_0d(i,j)^2}\qquad(i,j\in I),$$ and that, whenever $r(S)\le B_0\log n$, $$\label{eq:block-lens-input}
 \mu\left(\bigcap_{i\in S}C_i\right)
 \ge e^{-L(1+r(S)+\log|S|)}\bar c_{i(S)}.$$ Then there is a partition $\mathcal P$ of $I$ and measurable sets $E_S\subseteq\bigcap_{i\in S}C_i$, $S\in\mathcal P$, of measures $\lambda_S=\mu(E_S)$ such that $$\label{eq:block-anchor-loss}
 n^{-K}\bar c_{i(S)}\le\lambda_S\le\frac12.$$ Writing $q_S=-\log\lambda_S$ and $$w_{ST}=
 \frac{\mu(E_S\cap E_T)}
 {\sqrt{\lambda_S(1+\lambda_S)\lambda_T(1+\lambda_T)}},$$ we have $$\begin{aligned}
 \sum_{S\in\mathcal P}q_S
 &\le\sum_{i\in I}s_i+\frac{m}{10},
 \label{eq:block-log-cost}\\
 \sum_{S\in\mathcal P}\log(1+\lambda_S^{-1})
 &\le C\left(m+\sum_{i\in I}s_i\right),
 \label{eq:block-first-budget}\\
 \sum_{S\in\mathcal P}
 \log\left(1+\sum_{T\in\mathcal P\setminus\{S\}}w_{ST}\right)
 &\le C\left(m+\sum_{i\in I}s_i\right).
 \label{eq:block-row-budget}
\end{aligned}$$

For positive-measure slot lists and the diagrams in <a href="#eq:diagram-definition" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:diagram-definition">[eq:diagram-definition]</a>, the following bounds hold. For one witness type, suppress its label and set $$\delta_i=1+\lambda_i^{-1},\qquad
 w_{ij}=\frac{\mu(E_i\cap E_j)}
 {\sqrt{\lambda_i(1+\lambda_i)\lambda_j(1+\lambda_j)}}
 \quad(i\ne j).$$ Then $$1+\frac{D_{\alpha\alpha}}{\nu_\alpha^2}
 \le\prod_i\delta_i\prod_i\left(1+\sum_{j\ne i}w_{ij}\right).
 \label{eq:self-diagram-product}$$ For two witness types, put $$\rho_{\alpha\beta}=
 \sum_{i\in I_\alpha,j\in I_\beta}
 \frac{\mu(E_{\alpha i}\cap E_{\beta j})}
      {\lambda_{\alpha i}\lambda_{\beta j}}.$$ Then $$D_{\alpha\beta}\le\nu_\alpha\nu_\beta
                         (e^{\rho_{\alpha\beta}}-1).
 \label{eq:cross-diagram-product}$$ In particular, if every cross-slot intersection has measure zero, then $D_{\alpha\beta}=0$.

Let $n\ge2$, let $D,L>0$, and let $G\subset B_D$ be measurable with $|G|\ge\omega_nD^n/4$. Let $Y,Z$ be independent uniform points of $G$, and let $p,q\in\mathbb R^n$. For $0<\xi<\pi/2$, $$\begin{aligned}
&\mathbb P\bigl(0<|Y-p|\le L,\ 0<|Z-q|\le L,
                 \ \angle(Y-p,Z-q)\le\xi\bigr)
\notag\\[-2pt]
&\hspace{30mm}\le
 8\frac{\omega_{n-1}}{\omega_n}
       \left(\frac{L\sin\xi}{D}\right)^{n-1}
 \le 8\sqrt n\left(\frac{L\sin\xi}{D}\right)^{n-1}.
\label{eq:cylinder}
\end{aligned}$$
